    industries: ["SaaS & Software", "Startups", "Fintech", "Healthcare IT", "Ecommerce", "Media Technology", "Enterprise Software", "EdTech", "Logistics Technology"],
    tools: ["Docker", "GitHub Actions", "AWS EC2", "AWS ECS", "AWS RDS", "AWS S3", "AWS CloudFront", "Terraform", "Cloudflare", "Nginx", "Redis", "PostgreSQL", "Datadog", "Grafana", "Prometheus", "Let's Encrypt"],
    faq: [
      { q: "What is CI/CD and does my company need it?", a: "CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Deployment) is the automation that tests your code and deploys it to production reliably and repeatedly. If your team manually deploys code, tests deployments on production, has deployment-related incidents, or finds that deployments take more than 15 minutes, you need CI/CD. It's not just for large companies — even a 3-person startup benefits enormously from a proper deployment pipeline." },
      { q: "Do you work with existing AWS setups or only new infrastructure?", a: "Both. We commonly work with existing AWS accounts that have grown organically and need proper organization — cleaning up unused resources, implementing Terraform for what exists, fixing IAM permissions, and adding proper CI/CD. For greenfield projects, we design and provision infrastructure from scratch. We always audit before recommending changes." },
      { q: "Do you use Kubernetes or Docker Compose?", a: "Docker Compose for local development (essentially every project). For production orchestration, our default recommendation is AWS ECS (simpler to operate than Kubernetes for most application scales) or Vercel/AWS App Runner for stateless services. We implement Kubernetes (EKS) for workloads that genuinely need it — but we won't recommend it for a 3-service application just because it's technically interesting." },
      { q: "How do you handle secrets like database passwords and API keys?", a: "Never in code, never in environment variables committed to repositories, and never hardcoded in CI configuration files. We configure AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store for all sensitive values, with IAM role-based access so only the services that need a secret can access it. Secrets rotation is set up for database credentials. This eliminates the most common credential exposure vulnerability in deployment pipelines." },
      { q: "What happens to our existing infrastructure during the migration?", a: "We always run new infrastructure in parallel with existing before any cutover. For CI/CD migration, we build the new pipeline alongside the old process, validate it works correctly on staging, then switch over when everything is verified. Your current process continues working until the new pipeline is confirmed reliable. There's no 'big bang' switchover that puts production at risk." },
      { q: "Can you set up monitoring and alerting for our infrastructure?", a: "Yes — observability is a core part of every DevOps engagement. We configure infrastructure and application metrics collection (Datadog, Prometheus, or CloudWatch), build dashboards for key metrics (response times, error rates, resource utilization, deployment frequency), and set up alerting with appropriate severity levels and notification routing — so on-call engineers receive actionable alerts, not alert fatigue." },
      { q: "What cloud providers do you work with?", a: "AWS is our primary expertise and where we do the most work. We also work with Cloudflare (CDN, DDoS, DNS, Workers), Vercel (Next.js deployment and edge), and GitHub Actions (CI/CD) across all cloud environments. We have experience with GCP and Azure infrastructure but our deepest expertise is in AWS." },
      { q: "How long does it take to set up a CI/CD pipeline?", a: "A basic CI/CD pipeline with testing, Docker build, and deployment to staging and production for a single application typically takes 1–2 weeks. More comprehensive engagements including full infrastructure-as-code, multiple services, observability stack setup, and documentation typically run 3–5 weeks. We scope precisely before starting so you know what you're getting and when." },
      { q: "Will you document what you build so our team can maintain it?", a: "Documentation is non-negotiable. Every engagement includes: architecture diagrams, runbooks for common operational tasks (deploying a release, rolling back, adding a new service), incident response guides, and a knowledge transfer session with your engineering team. You should be able to maintain and extend everything we build without us." },
      { q: "How do we get started?", a: "Contact us via the form below or WhatsApp +91 88140 12395. For DevOps engagements, the first step is a free infrastructure audit — we'll review your current deployment process, AWS setup, and engineering workflows to identify the highest-leverage improvements and scope the engagement accurately. No commitment required for the audit conversation." },
    ],
